      "problem": "$|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|=$",
      "worked_solution": "The $1$st row of the matrix contains three zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+1\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
